            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the German for written, then the German for the school report."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: pauken",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pauken — \"to cram, to swot (informal)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Vor der Prüfung hat sie jeden Abend Vokabeln gepaukt. Before the exam she crammed vocabulary every evening."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Informal; büffeln means the same. In Austria people also say stucken."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pauken is built on Pauke \"kettledrum\", from Middle High German pūke (EtymDB). The school sense is usually explained as drumming facts into your head."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
